@@ -0,0 +1,172 @@
+.TH LLAPI_FIND_SINCE 3 2026-08-28 "Lustre User API" "Lustre Library Functions"
+.SH NAME
+llapi_find_since \- run lfs find's predicates over what a changelog says changed
+.SH SYNOPSIS
+.nf
+.B #include <lustre/lustreapi.h>
+.PP
+.BI "int llapi_find_since(const char *" path ,
+.BI "                     struct find_param *" param ");"
+.PP
+.fi
+.SH DESCRIPTION
+.B llapi_find_since()
+answers the search in
+.I param
+over the objects a changelog reports as changed, rather than over every
+object a walk of
+.I path
+would reach. The answer is the same answer, from fewer objects: a changelog
+says which objects had an event, each one is then verified through the
+mount, and the predicates that judge it are the ones
+.BR lfs-find (1)
+applies to a walk. An object the log names but the predicates reject is not
+in the answer, so this is a subset of the plain search and never a different
+set.
+.LP
+.I path
+names the subtree to search, as it does for a walk. A changelog is per\-MDT
+and covers the whole filesystem, so the subtree is enforced against each
+object's resolved pathname; an object with more than one link is in the
+answer if any of its names is under
+.IR path ,
+and is reported once, under the first of them that matches. A walk reaches
+such a file once per directory entry and prints it once per link, so the
+objects answered for are a subset of the same search without an anchor while
+the lines printed are not a subset of its lines.
+.SS Where to start
+.I fp_since_kind
+says what the anchor is, and the two are not interchangeable:
+.TP 20
+.B LLAPI_SINCE_TIME
+.I fp_since_time
+is an absolute time, and every MDT's log is read from its beginning and cut
+at that instant. This is the form that works on any filesystem.
+.TP
+.B LLAPI_SINCE_INDEX
+.I fp_since_index
+is a
+.I cr_index
+to resume from. An index belongs to one MDT, so this is refused with
+.B -EINVAL
+on a filesystem with more than one rather than answered from MDT0000 alone,
+which would be a short answer with nothing to say so.
+.TP
+.B LLAPI_SINCE_NONE
+no anchor, which is refused: there is nothing to search since.
+.SS What it needs
+Every MDT of the filesystem is read in turn. Reading a changelog needs no
+registered user, but having anything to read does: an MDT with no user
+records nothing at all \(em
+.B mdd_changelog_off()
+clears
+.B CLM_ON
+when the last one deregisters, and
+.B mdd_changelog_enabled()
+requires it \(em and the mask a user was registered with decides which
+events are recorded while there is one. See
+.BR lfs-changelog (1)
+and
+.BR lctl (8).
+.LP
+Resolving each candidate's FID to a pathname needs whatever
+.BR llapi_fid2path (3)
+needs \(em
+.B CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH
+unless
+.B llite.*.user_fid2path
+is set. A refusal there does not end the search: the errno is kept and
+returned once the whole run is over, that object is left out of the answer,
+and how many were dropped that way is reported at the end. Set
+.I fp_stop_on_error
+to have the first one end the run instead.
+.LP
+An object that has been unlinked since its event no longer resolves and is
+not in the answer. How many were dropped that way is reported once when the
+search ends, so a shorter answer than expected says so rather than being
+silent.
+.SH RETURN VALUES
+.B llapi_find_since()
+returns:
+.TP
+0
+on success.
+.TP
+<0
+a negative errno on failure.
+.SH ERRORS
+.TP 15
+.B -EINVAL
+.I path
+or
+.I param
+is NULL,
+.I fp_since_kind
+is
+.BR LLAPI_SINCE_NONE ,
+or it is
+.B LLAPI_SINCE_INDEX
+on a filesystem with more than one MDT.
+.TP 15
+.B -ENOTSUP
+.I fp_thread_count
+is above 1. This source is single-threaded, and the count is tested as soon
+as the arguments are well formed \(em before
+.I path
+is resolved or any MDT is read \(em so a
+.B find_param
+that a walk left a thread count in is refused rather than run on one thread.
+.TP 15
+.B -ENODEV
+no MDT of that filesystem is visible from this client, or
+.I path
+names no Lustre mount \(em
+.BR llapi_search_fsname (3)
+and
+.BR llapi_search_mounts (3)
+both end in the same lookup, which answers this.
+.TP 15
+.B -ENOENT
+.I path
+does not resolve: this is
+.BR realpath (3)'s
+answer, and not the one for a path outside Lustre.
+.TP 15
+.B -ENAMETOOLONG
+.I path
+as the caller spelled it does not fit.
+.TP 15
+.B -ENOMEM
+the MDT list or the search's own buffers could not be allocated.
+.TP 15
+.B -EPERM
+FIDs cannot be resolved through this mount.
+.LP
+An error reading an MDT's changelog is returned as it came.
+.SH EXAMPLES
+Every regular file changed in the last two hours, under
+.IR /mnt/lustre/data :
+.EX
+        struct find_param param = { 0 };
+        int rc;
+\&
+        param.fp_max_depth   = (unsigned int)-1;
+        param.fp_type        = S_IFREG;
+        param.fp_since_kind  = LLAPI_SINCE_TIME;
+        param.fp_since_time  = time(NULL) - 2 * 60 * 60;
+\&
+        rc = llapi_find_since("/mnt/lustre/data", &param);
+.EE
+.SH AVAILABILITY
+.B llapi_find_since()
+is part of the
+.BR lustre (7)
+user application interface library since release 2.18.0.
+.\" Added in commit v2_17_57-xxx-gxxxxxxxxx
+.SH SEE ALSO
+.BR lfs-changelog (1),
+.BR lfs-find (1),
+.BR llapi_scan_changelog (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_fid (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_namespace (3),
+.BR lustreapi (7)
